  "query_preview": "..\nAreas of valley fog should stay mainly light through about 12Z\ntoday with vsbys AOA 2SM.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Severe thunderstorms capable of damaging winds \nand heavy rainfall are possible this afternoon and Tuesday\n\n\nThere is still some uncertainty regarding the coverage of \nstorms during the day today as high res models have quite a \nwide spread w/respect to the timing, location and apparent \nintensity of several small areas of preferred CI and movement of\nthe individual cells or clusters of SHRA/TSRA. \n\nOn the larger scale we're looking at weak ridging sliding over\nthe state from the NW at the surface and aloft today, which \nwill almost certainly create an unfavorable environment for \nanything more than a stray shower/TSRA through 19Z if not longer\ntoday. \n\nWe should see at least scattered thunderstorm development \nduring the afternoon as modest instability develops, but shear \nwill remain weak. The severe threat appears to be low, but a few\nstrong to severe wind gusts will be possible with any stronger \nstorms. \n\nSome models still continue to highlight a MCS diving south/SE  \nfrom the Great Lakes later tonight which could impact western \nportions of the area, but confidence is low on timing and \nplacement of this feature.\n\nTuesday will likely see the most widespread showers and\nthunderstorms of the week coming in one or two distinct rounds. \n\nA potent shortwave approaches from the west and its associated \nsurface low tracks over the region. Showers and storms will \nlikely be ongoing across parts of Central PA Tuesday morning and\nshould continue through the rest of the day. The potential for \nsevere weather remains uncertain as it will depend on much we \nare able to destabilize behind the morning showers and storms. \nSPC has areas roughly along and south of I-80 in a marginal risk\nfor severe weather and they have included far southern portions\nof the area in a slight risk.\n\nHeavy rain will also be a concern this evening into Tuesday \nwith any storms that develop as PWAT values will climb to around\n1.8 inches. Storms will be very efficient rainfall producers \nand areas that see repeated rounds of thunderstorms may see some\nflash flooding concerns develop. WPC has placed nearly all of \nCentral PA in a marginal risk for excessive rainfall for both \nMonday and Tuesday.",
